# 4. THE CENTRAL 3D WORLD

The 3D environment is the most important feature of the entire product.

It must be a real interactive Three.js scene, not a static image, background video or fake 3D illustration.

## Camera behavior

Implement a smooth, unrestricted horizontal orbit around the world, covering the full 360 degrees.

The user must be able to:

- Rotate left and right around the entire world.
- Tilt the camera vertically within sensible limits.
- Zoom in to inspect decorations.
- Zoom out to see the entire world.
- Return to a default camera position.
- Inspect the environment from different angles.

Use smooth damping and avoid abrupt camera movement.

Prevent the camera from entering the ground or flipping underneath the scene.

Set sensible zoom limits to prevent the user from getting lost or clipping through objects.

Desktop controls:

- Left mouse drag: orbit.
- Mouse wheel: zoom.
- Object click: select an object.
- Ground click: place or reposition an object when the corresponding mode is active.

Mobile controls:

- One-finger drag: orbit.
- Pinch: zoom.
- Tap an object: select it.
- Tap the ground: place or move an object.

Do not allow object-placement mode to make camera rotation unusable. Make the interaction mode clear and provide an easy way to cancel it.

## Ground and terrain

Create a three-dimensional snowy plot with enough room for houses, trees, roads, lights and decorations.

The environment should have:

- A snow-covered ground plane or terrain mesh.
- Subtle elevation variation where appropriate.
- Snow materials with convincing roughness and soft highlights.
- Natural-looking terrain edges.
- Atmospheric depth.
- Shadows beneath objects.
- Optional low snowbanks around the perimeter.
- A visually coherent scale for all assets.

Avoid a featureless, perfectly flat white plane as the final visual direction.

Use procedural terrain where useful, but organize the implementation so that better terrain meshes and materials can replace the prototype later.

## Lighting and atmosphere

Create a cinematic winter lighting setup.

Use:

- A cool blue-white ambient environment.
- A directional light representing winter sunlight or moonlight, depending on the selected atmosphere.
- Soft shadows.
- Atmospheric fog with carefully tuned distance.
- Warm emissive window materials.
- Small warm light sources around lamps and Christmas decorations.
- A restrained bloom effect only if performance remains acceptable.
- A high-quality sky or environment map when available.
- Optional light snowfall.

The lighting must preserve detail in the snow instead of clipping it to pure white.

Warm lights should contrast beautifully with the cool winter environment.

Use post-processing sparingly. The game must remain responsive on ordinary modern laptops and mobile devices.

## Snowfall

Snowfall is optional for the first functional milestone but desirable.

If implemented:

- Use an efficient particle system or instanced geometry.
- Avoid creating hundreds or thousands of independent React components.
- Make the snowfall visually subtle.
- Respect performance and reduced-motion preferences where practical.
- Keep the snow particles separate from the world-saving model.

