# 7. OBJECT PLACEMENT, SELECTION AND EDITING

Every placement action must work in the actual 3D scene.

## Placement workflow

1. The user opens the object panel.
2. The user chooses an object.
3. The interface clearly indicates that placement mode is active.
4. The user clicks or taps a valid position on the snowy ground.
5. The object appears at the corresponding world coordinates.
6. The object is selected and visual feedback confirms the placement.
7. The user can place additional objects or exit placement mode.

Use a raycasting approach or React Three Fiber pointer events to determine the position on the terrain.

Objects must not appear at arbitrary screen coordinates or at unrelated positions.

## Selection

When the user selects an object:

- Display a subtle selection outline, ring or ground marker.
- Show its object type.
- Offer relevant editing actions.
- Keep the selection visually clear without making the world look like a CAD editor.

## Movement

Allow the user to reposition any placed object.

A reliable initial interaction is:

1. Select an object.
2. Click “Move”.
3. Click the new position on the ground.
4. The object moves to that position.

A later improvement may add direct drag-and-drop movement with a ground-constrained pointer.

Never move an object accidentally when the user intended to rotate the camera.

## Rotation

Allow users to rotate selected objects around their vertical axis.

Provide simple rotation controls, such as:

- Rotate left.
- Rotate right.
- Reset rotation.

Use sensible increments initially, with optional finer controls later.

## Deletion

Allow users to remove a selected object after an explicit action.

Do not delete an object just because the user clicks the ground or switches modes.

## Placement validation

Keep placed objects inside the buildable area.

Prevent objects from being placed outside the terrain or at impossible coordinates.

If two objects overlap significantly, provide reasonable feedback or allow the user to correct the position. Avoid complex collision restrictions that make creative building frustrating.

The user should have considerable creative freedom.

