# 22. TESTING AND ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA

Do not mark a feature complete merely because its code exists.

Verify behavior.

## 3D world tests

- The scene renders without console errors.
- All four landscapes can be selected.
- The camera rotates through 360 degrees.
- Zoom works within limits.
- The world remains visually coherent at different camera angles.

## Placement tests

- Selecting an object activates placement mode.
- Clicking the ground places the object at the correct world position.
- The object can be selected.
- The object can be moved.
- The object can be rotated.
- The object can be deleted.
- The object remains within the buildable area.
- Changing the landscape does not unexpectedly erase user-created objects.

## Countdown tests

- The countdown updates.
- The next Christmas is calculated correctly.
- The date boundary around December 25 is handled correctly.

## Gift tests

- The gift panel opens.
- The available choices are visible.
- A chosen gift is added to the world.
- The updated world is saved.
- Production reward limits cannot be bypassed by refreshing or manipulating client state.

## Persistence tests

- Refreshing restores the saved world.
- Logging out and back in restores the correct user's world.
- A failed network request does not silently destroy valid saved data.
- Users cannot access other users' worlds.

## Export tests

- PNG export downloads an image.
- The image contains the actual 3D world.
- Export works with the selected landscape and placed objects.
- Export errors are handled visibly.

## Build tests

Run the appropriate checks, including:

- TypeScript compilation.
- Production build.
- Relevant unit tests.
- Browser-level interaction tests where available.

Fix the errors before reporting success.

Do not invent test results. Clearly distinguish tests that were executed from tests that remain unverified.

